# Files excluded from the Vercel deployment. They stay in git; they are simply
# never uploaded, so requesting one returns a genuine 404 served by 404.html.
#
# WHY THIS FILE RATHER THAN vercel.json
#   Vercel `redirects` emit a 3xx and `rewrites` serve the target with a 200 —
#   neither produces a 404. Legacy `routes` supports "status": 404 but cannot be
#   combined with a `headers` array, which we need. Excluding a file from the
#   upload is the only mechanism that actually enforces a public 404 on Vercel.
#
# This replaces _redirects (Netlify/Cloudflare). .htaccess and web.config cover
# the same ground for Apache and IIS.
#
# .github/scripts/check_headers.py fails the build if a non-asset file appears
# in the web root without being listed here.

# --- Repository tooling ---------------------------------------------------
.github/
scripts/
docs/
node_modules/
.git/
.claude/

# --- Tooling manifests ----------------------------------------------------
# The site has no runtime dependencies and no build step, so Vercel needs none
# of these. Excluding package.json means Vercel treats the project as static
# plus the /api functions, which is exactly right — but confirm on the first
# preview deploy that /api/contact still responds.
package.json
package-lock.json
.htmlvalidate.json
.pa11yci

# --- Documentation and internal notes -------------------------------------
README.md
SECURITY.md
website-production-checklist.md

# --- Source archives — these must never be downloadable --------------------
*.zip
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.bak
*.old
*.sql
*.log

# --- Host configs for the other platforms ---------------------------------
_headers
_redirects
.htaccess
web.config
.gitignore
